    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female named Frances presents to the clinic with injuries highly suspicious for physical abuse. Which of the following represents the most appropriate documentation standard for the clinician in the medical record?', '["Estimate the sizes and locations of the bruises from memory after the patient has been discharged", "Write a brief note stating ''bruises present, suspect fall'' to keep the record simple and concise", "Use subjective language such as ''caregiver is definitely guilty'' and ''patient is lying'' to help the police case", "Write detailed, objective descriptions of all injuries, use anatomical diagrams to mark sizes and locations, document the patient''s quotes verbatim, and obtain consent to take high-quality photographs of the lesions", "Avoid writing any details about the suspected abuse in the chart to prevent legal exposure for the hospital"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Meticulous and objective medical documentation is critical in elder abuse cases because the medical record serves as a legal document in civil or criminal proceedings. Proper documentation includes: (1) **Detailed, objective descriptions** of the size, shape, color, and location of all injuries. (2) **Anatomical body maps/diagrams**. (3) **Verbatim quotes** from the patient and caregiver regarding how the injuries occurred. (4) **High-quality photographs** of the injuries (after obtaining the patient''s or surrogate''s consent). Subjective or accusatory language should be avoided. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Lachs & Pillemer, N Engl J Med 2015)'),
