    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old male named Richard with advanced dementia is admitted to a long-term care facility. The facility staff asks the physician to order bilateral wrist restraints and four side rails up on the bed to prevent the patient from wandering and falling. According to geriatric principles and federal guidelines, what is the most appropriate clinical response?', '["Refuse the request; physical restraints and four side rails do not reduce falls, increase the risk of serious injury (e.g., strangulation, entrapment), and worsen functional decline; recommend non-pharmacological alternatives", "Order a continuous infusion of intravenous haloperidol as a safer chemical alternative to physical restraints", "Approve the order immediately, as physical restraints are the gold standard for preventing falls in patients with wandering behavior", "Instruct the family that they must sit by the patient''s bed 24 hours a day if they refuse the physical restraints", "Approve the order but instruct the staff to release the restraints only when state inspectors are present in the building"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Geriatric clinical guidelines and federal regulations (OBRA 1987) strongly discourage the use of physical restraints (e.g., wrist ties, vest restraints) and four side rails. Studies show that **restraints do not reduce the incidence of falls or injuries**; instead, they increase morbidity and mortality from **strangulation, entrapment, pressure injuries, deconditioning, and agitation**. Sparing side rails and using low-height beds, floor mats, bed alarms, and structured activities are safer, non-pharmacological alternatives. Restraints can only be used under strict medical necessity, never for staff convenience. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 82; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old female named Frances with advanced dementia is admitted to a long-term care facility. The facility staff asks the physician to order bilateral wrist restraints and four side rails up on the bed to prevent the patient from wandering and falling. According to geriatric principles and federal guidelines, what is the most appropriate clinical response?', '["Instruct the family that they must sit by the patient''s bed 24 hours a day if they refuse the physical restraints", "Order a continuous infusion of intravenous haloperidol as a safer chemical alternative to physical restraints", "Approve the order immediately, as physical restraints are the gold standard for preventing falls in patients with wandering behavior", "Refuse the request; physical restraints and four side rails do not reduce falls, increase the risk of serious injury (e.g., strangulation, entrapment), and worsen functional decline; recommend non-pharmacological alternatives", "Approve the order but instruct the staff to release the restraints only when state inspectors are present in the building"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Geriatric clinical guidelines and federal regulations (OBRA 1987) strongly discourage the use of physical restraints (e.g., wrist ties, vest restraints) and four side rails. Studies show that **restraints do not reduce the incidence of falls or injuries**; instead, they increase morbidity and mortality from **strangulation, entrapment, pressure injuries, deconditioning, and agitation**. Sparing side rails and using low-height beds, floor mats, bed alarms, and structured activities are safer, non-pharmacological alternatives. Restraints can only be used under strict medical necessity, never for staff convenience. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 82; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
