    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old female named Irene is seen in the clinic. The clinician files a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) based on reasonable suspicion of caregiver neglect. The caregiver learns of the report and threatens to sue the clinician for defamation and breach of confidentiality. Which of the following legal protections applies to the clinician in this scenario?', '["The clinician is protected only if they obtain a written waiver from the caregiver prior to filing the report", "The clinician will be suspended from practice until a court verifies that the abuse actually took place", "Clinicians are immune from lawsuits only if they report the abuse anonymously without providing their names", "The clinician has no legal protection and can be sued for damages if the APS investigation fails to prove abuse occurred", "State laws provide civil and criminal immunity to mandated reporters who file elder abuse reports in good faith, protecting them from liability and lawsuits"]'::jsonb, 4, 'To encourage reporting and protect vulnerable populations, all US states have statutes that grant **civil and criminal immunity to mandated reporters** who file reports of suspected elder abuse, neglect, or exploitation **in good faith**. This immunity protects clinicians from lawsuits alleging defamation, libel, or breach of confidentiality (HIPAA specifically exempts reports required by state law), even if the subsequent APS investigation determines that no abuse occurred. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
