    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old male named Kenneth is seen for a routine wellness visit. Which of the following patient and caregiver characteristic combinations carries the highest statistical risk for the development of elder abuse or neglect?', '["A patient with controlled hypertension who visits adult day care programs three times a week", "A patient with diabetes living with an adult daughter who is a professional nurse and has extensive social support", "A patient with mild osteoarthritis living with a supportive spouse who attends caregiver support groups", "A patient with intact cognitive function living independently in a retirement community with daily home health aides", "A patient with advanced dementia and high physical dependence, cared for by a co-residing relative who has active substance abuse, untreated mental illness, or high caregiver stress"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The risk of elder abuse is multifactorial, but the **highest risk combination** occurs when: (1) the **patient has severe cognitive impairment (dementia)** and is highly dependent for ADLs, (2) the **caregiver lives with the patient**, and (3) the **caregiver has active substance abuse, untreated mental illness, or severe caregiver burnout**. Social isolation of the household is also a major contributing factor. Identifying these high-risk dyads allows clinicians to implement preventive support (respite care, counseling, home visits). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Lachs & Pillemer, N Engl J Med 2015; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
